  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Tuesday)\n\nSubtropical Ridge of high pressure remains in place over the \ncentral Rockies of Wyoming and Colorado. Meanwhile an inverted \ntrough is undercutting the upper ridge, this trough currently near\nthe Rio Grande valley will continue to move west into Arizona \nTuesday. The two systems will keep southerly flow and the surge of\nmonsoonal moisture over Nevada with near record precipitable \nwater values leading to a high chance for scattered thunderstorms \nagain Tuesday afternoon. Storms hazards will include heavy \nrainfall, gusty outflow winds, hail, and localized flash flooding.\nGiven the moist environment with the mid level moisture surge, \nthere is a slight risk for excessive rainfall as storm motion \nallows for slow moving and training of cells over any given \nlocation. A flash flood watch remains in effect for Elko, Eureka, \nLander, N Nye, and White Pine Counties through Tuesday.\nWednesday, the trough will be rounding the western edge of the \nridge and lifting north into Utah, setting up another active day \nWednesday afternoon, although not as active as Tuesday. By \nThursday the trough get picked up by the westerly flow over the \nnorthern tier of the US. The ridge will also be shifting to the SW\nas well, bad news is this will cut of the monsoonal flow for a \nwhile, allowing temperatures to warm up while storm chance dwindle\nover the weekend, before ending Monday of next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for scattered thunderstorms with heavy \nrainfall Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons.\n\nThere moderate-high confidence in the potential for flash \nflooding issues for northeastern Nevada for Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith a flash flood watch in effect.",
